# /etc/inittab — BusyBox init, PID 1 on b1nix.
#
# The kernel's default init is /sbin/init (kernel/main.c, M94), which is a
# symlink onto the BusyBox multicall ELF — Alpine's layout. This file is what
# PID 1 reads, so it describes every ordinary boot, not a special mode.
#
# The split is Alpine's too: BusyBox init is the *supervisor* — it reaps
# orphans, runs the sysinit and shutdown phases and respawns getty — while
# OpenRC stays the *high-level* init system that owns the service graph
# (/etc/init.d + /etc/runlevels, M94) and drives every runlevel below it.
#
# OpenRC's own PID 1, /sbin/openrc-init, is still a real bootable
# configuration, selected with the M94 cmdline parameter:
#
#     init=/sbin/openrc-init
#
# It is worth keeping because it owns the /run/openrc/init.ctl control channel
# that openrc-shutdown and telinit talk to, which BusyBox init has no
# equivalent of. Both inits run the same services out of the same files.
#
# Format:  <id>:<runlevels>:<action>:<process>   (id/runlevels unused by BusyBox)

# Bring the system up through OpenRC's own runlevels, in OpenRC's order.
::sysinit:/sbin/openrc sysinit
::sysinit:/sbin/openrc boot
::wait:/sbin/openrc default

# Test hook: only does anything when the kernel cmdline carries b1nix.test=1
# AND b1nix.smoke=init (see the script). On an ordinary boot it exits at once.
::wait:/bin/sh /etc/init-smoke.sh

# Diagnostic hook: only does anything when the kernel cmdline carries
# b1nix.swayprobe (see the script). On an ordinary boot it exits at once, so
# this costs a fork and nothing else.
::wait:/bin/sh /etc/sway-probe.sh

# Diagnostic hook: only does anything when the kernel cmdline carries
# b1nix.i915sway — sway on the passed-through GPU's real output.
::wait:/bin/sh /etc/i915-sway.sh

# Both composition paths, each proved on its own. On a test boot the gfx smoke
# runs this from /etc/local.d/00-smoke.start; here it is reachable on an image
# that carries a real GL driver (make B1NIX_GPU_DRV=1) with b1nix.render-smoke.
::wait:/bin/sh -c 'grep -q b1nix.render-smoke /proc/cmdline 2>/dev/null && exec /bin/sh /etc/render-smoke.sh || true'
# KDE's compositor, on b1nix.kde. Gated inside the script like the others.
::wait:/bin/sh /etc/kde.sh

# Soak hook: only does anything when the kernel cmdline carries b1nix.soak=<list>
# — the overnight stress instance (see /etc/soak.sh and tools/soak/).
::wait:/bin/sh /etc/soak.sh

# Diagnostic hook: only does anything when the kernel cmdline carries
# b1nix.chromium-min — the smallest browser case, with no compositor and no GPU.
::wait:/bin/sh /etc/chromium-min.sh

# Interactive login on the first virtual terminal. The serial line (/dev/ttyS0)
# deliberately gets no getty: it is the kernel console, and a getty there would
# claim it as its controlling terminal and take the foreground process group
# away from whatever is already running on it.
::respawn:/bin/getty -L 0 tty1 linux

# A login on the serial line, for the boots that ask for one.
#
# The serial port is the kernel console, so a getty there fights the log and
# every automated run would have one — hence the gate. `b1nix.serial-getty`
# turns it on for the case the gate exists for: driving the machine by hand
# from the terminal that started QEMU.
::respawn:/bin/sh -c 'grep -q b1nix.serial-getty /proc/cmdline && exec /bin/getty -L 115200 ttyS0 vt100 || exec /bin/sleep 86400'

# Ctrl-Alt-Del and shutdown handling.
::ctrlaltdel:/sbin/reboot
::shutdown:/sbin/openrc shutdown
::restart:/sbin/init
